Curling Iron vs Curling Wand - Here's What You Need To Know
Whether you choose a curling iron or a curling wand, you'll love the results you can achieve at home, which is exactly why these two styling tools are so popular among beauty enthusiasts. Both tools offer a wide range of heat control features, multiple barrel sizes, and even interchangeable barrels. We have narrowed down some of the most important differences that can help guide you toward the best curling iron vs curling wand for you. If you want a curling iron, here's everything you need to know. And if you're looking for a curling wand like the one we just reviewed, you'll want to read the rest of this article next: Curling wands vs Curling irons: What's the Difference for Long Hair? While there is some overlap between these hot styling tools, they aren’t quite the same. A curling iron is a versatile, styling tool that comes in fixed or adjustable barrel sizes and multiple heat settings for precise styling. The most common types of curling irons are ceramic, tourmaline, titanium, and gold. The most common benefits of a curling iron is the ability to quickly create curls, waves, or volume in the hair with a variety of sizes and heat settings. The heat settings will determine whether you use high, low, medium, or auto settings, depending on where you want to leave your hair. A curling iron can be used to make every hair style you need, from loose curls to tight ringlets. That means it can be used to make both up-do hair styles for formal occasions as well as simple beachy waves and curls for everyday use. Many women prefer hair curlers because they can easily be used anywhere, be it the bathroom, the beach, or even at a job interview. However, curling irons can be time consuming and require more maintenance as compared to a curling wand since they must be cleaned, heated, and cooled off before and after each use. Curling irons come in two basic types: flat irons and barrel curling irons. Flat irons have a flat, smooth surface with a small opening (about 2 or 3 mm) 